WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:11.550
Today we are going to generate human faces,
and even better, we will keep them intact.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.710
You will see what that means in a moment.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:20.310
This new neural network-based technique can
dream up completely new images, and more.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:26.070
However, this is not the first technique to
do this, but this…this does them better.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:32.090
Let’s look at 3 amazing features that it
offers, and then discuss, how, and why it

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.829
is better than its predecessors.

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:40.960
Hold on to your papers for the first example,
which is my favorite, image toonification.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.320
Would you like to see what the AI thinks you
would look like if you were a Disney character?

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.550
Well, here you go.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:54.730
And these are not some rudimentary, first-paper-in-the-works
kind of results.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:59.590
These are proper toonifications, you could
ask for money for some of these, and they

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:03.959
are done completely automatically by a learning
algorithm.

00:01:03.959 --> 00:01:04.989
Woah.

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:11.600
At the end of the video, you will also witness
as I myself get toonified.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.950
And what is even cooler is that we can not
only produce these still images, but even

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:23.070
compute intermediate images between two input
photos, and get meaningful results.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:30.100
I’ll stop the process here and there to
show you how good these are.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.150
I am blown away.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:35.950
Two, it can also perform the usual suspects.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:45.189
For instance, it can make us older or younger,
or put a smile on our face too.

00:01:45.189 --> 00:01:55.149
However, three it works not only on human
faces, but cars, animals, and buildings too.

00:01:55.149 --> 00:02:00.789
So the results are all great, but how does
all this wizardry happen?

00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:08.090
Well, we take an image, embed it into a latent
space, and in this space, we can easily apply

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:09.090
modifications.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:13.530
Okay…but what is this latent space thing?

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:19.010
A latent space is a made-up place where we
are trying to organize data in a way that

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.170
similar things are close to each other.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:26.600
What you see here is a 2D latent space for
generating different fonts.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:33.100
It is hard to explain why these fonts are
similar, but most of us would agree that they

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.390
indeed share some common properties.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:41.560
The cool thing here is that we can explore
this latent space with our cursor, and generate

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.480
all kinds of new fonts.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.880
You can try this work in your browser, the
link is available in the video description.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:54.840
And, luckily, we can build a latent space,
not only for fonts, but, for nearly anything.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.760
I am a light transport researcher by trade,
so in this earlier paper, we were interested

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.330
in generating hundreds of variants of a material
model to populate this scene.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:11.280
In this latent space, we can concoct all of
these really cool digital material models.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.720
A link to this work is also available in the
video description.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:21.660
Now, for the face generation algorithms, this
embedding step is typically imperfect, which

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.160
means that we might lose some information
during the process.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.130
In the better cases, things may look a little
different, and that’s not even the worst

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:32.130
case scenario.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.420
I’ll show you that in a moment.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:40.260
For the milder case, here is an earlier example
from a paper by the name StyleFlow, where

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:46.780
the authors embedded me into a latent space
and it indeed came out a little different.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.130
But not so bad.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:55.300
A later work, StyleClip, was able to make
me look like Obi-Wan Kenobi, which is excellent.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:59.160
However, the embedding step was more imperfect.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.730
The bearded image was embedded like this.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:07.210
You are probably saying that this looks different,
but even this is not so bad.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:12.190
If you want to see a much worse example, look
at this.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:13.230
My goodness.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.480
Now this is quite different.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.560
Now that we saw what it could do, it is time
to ask the big question.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.070
How much better is it than previous works?

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:26.030
Do we have an A-B test for that?

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:28.200
And the answer is yes, of course!

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:34.070
Let’s embed this gentleman and see how he
comes out on the other end.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:39.050
Well, without the improvements of this paper,
once again, quite different.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.180
The beard is nearly gone.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:47.430
And when we toonify the image, let’s see…yup,
that beard is gone for good.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:51.030
So, can this paper get that beard back?

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:53.620
Let’s see…oh yes!

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:59.170
If we refine the embedding with the new method,
we get that glorious beard back.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.490
That is one heck of a toon image, congratulations!

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.130
Loving it.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:05.440
And now, it’s my turn.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:11.610
One of the results was amazing, I really like
this one.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:12.940
How about this one?

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:15.220
Well, not bad.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:19.390
And I wonder if it can deal with sunglasses?

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:24.310
Well, kind of, but not in the way you might
think.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:25.320
What do you think?

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.500
Let me know in the comments below!

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:32.840
Note that you can only see these results here
on Two Minute Papers, and a big thank you

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:39.000
to the authors for taking time off their busy
day and doing these experiments for us.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.440
And here are a few more tests, let’s see
how it fares with these.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.800
The inputs are a diverse set of images from
different classes, and the initial embeddings

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.120
are, well, a bit of a disappointment.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:57.110
But that is kind of the point, because this
new technique does not let it stop there and

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:05.150
iteratively improves them, yes, getting better,
and by the end, my goodness.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:06.780
Very close to the input.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:12.169
Don’t forget that the goal here is not to
implement a copying machine, the key difference

00:06:12.169 --> 00:06:17.780
is that we can’t do too much with the input
image, but after the embedding step, we can

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:23.900
do all this toonification and other kinds
magic with it, and the results are only relevant

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.450
as long as the two images are close.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.310
And they are really close.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.110
Bravo!

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:33.110
So good!

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:37.040
So, I hope that now you agree that the pace
of progress in machine learning and synthetic

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:40.590
image generation is absolutely incredible.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:07:31.630
What a time to be alive!

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:40.780
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

